---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { getPath } from '../../utils/paths';
import '../../styles/lab.css';

const real = [
  { piece: 'Kafka wire codec, every request and response, byte-exact', crate: 'krabka-protocol', use: 'every frame a simulated broker or client handles' },
  { piece: 'RecordBatch v2 codec with CRC-32C', crate: 'krabka-protocol::records', use: 'every partition log' },
  { piece: 'KRaft state machine (KIP-595, KIP-996)', crate: 'krabka-kraft-core', use: 'the controller quorum between brokers' },
  { piece: 'Metadata records and the immutable metadata image', crate: 'krabka-metadata', use: "the controller log and every broker's view of the cluster" },
  { piece: 'Streams topology, processors, state stores, changelogs', crate: 'krabka-client-streams', use: 'every streams application node' },
  { piece: 'Avro, JSON Schema and Protobuf engines', crate: 'apache-avro, jsonschema, prost-reflect', use: 'the schema registry' },
];

const controls = [
  { control: 'Play / Pause, +10 ms, +100 ms, +1 s, Settle, speed', effect: 'The page owns the clock. Nothing moves until it advances simulated time; Settle runs until nothing is due.' },
  { control: 'Drag a card', effect: 'Moves the node. Positions are saved with the scenario and shared with the link.' },
  { control: 'Click, Shift+click', effect: 'Selects a node; a second node selected with Shift enables the link faults for that pair.' },
  { control: 'Kill / Restart / Wipe', effect: 'Halts a node with its state kept, boots it again from that state, or boots it from nothing.' },
  { control: 'Isolate / Reconnect, Partition / Heal, Latency, Loss', effect: 'Cuts or restores every link of a node or one link between two, or sets that link’s latency and loss rate.' },
  { control: 'Right-click or long-press a card', effect: 'The context menu: edit, send a control command, faults, remove.' },
  { control: 'Inspector tabs', effect: 'State shows what the node reports (partitions, ISR, groups, lag, schemas); Config edits it and restarts the node; Raw JSON is the snapshot.' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Cluster Lab"
  description="Build a Krabka cluster in your browser: brokers, a schema registry, producers, consumers and streams apps in WebAssembly, with faults and multi-tab hosting."
  currentSlug="/docs/lab"
>
  <div class="space-y-6 font-sans">
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-white sm:text-4xl">Cluster Lab</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-400 mt-2 text-sm max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        A distributed-systems playground. Place brokers, clients and apps on a canvas, run the cluster, break it, and watch it recover. No backend, no install.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Every node on the canvas is a synchronous state machine compiled to <strong class="text-white">WebAssembly</strong>.
        It receives frames and timer ticks and emits frames; nothing in the module opens a socket, reads a clock or touches a
        file. The page owns the clock and the virtual network, so a scenario replays exactly from its seed, and every fault is a
        deliberate act, not an accident of timing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The brokers speak the real Kafka wire protocol to the real clients, and the durable state of every node (partition logs,
        the controller log, the registry's schemas) is kept in this browser's IndexedDB, so a reload reopens the cluster where you
        left it. Several tabs, on one machine or across the internet, can each host a share of the nodes over WebRTC data channels.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div id="krabka-lab" class="not-prose">
      <p class="lab-fallback">
        Loading the Cluster Lab. This panel needs JavaScript and WebAssembly. If it does not appear, your browser may have them disabled.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">What to try</h2>
      <p>
        Load <strong class="text-white">Three brokers, a producer, a consumer group</strong> from the presets and press
        <strong class="text-white">Settle</strong>. The brokers hold a KRaft election, the controller creates the topic, the
        producer's records flow through a replicated partition to the two consumers. Select the broker the inspector names as
        controller and <strong class="text-white">Kill</strong> it: leadership moves, the consumers keep reading, and the
        acked records are still there when you <strong class="text-white">Restart</strong> it. Shift+click two brokers and
        <strong class="text-white">Partition</strong> them, then heal the link and watch the ISR shrink and grow back.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white">Network probe</strong> runs on two diagnostic node kinds, an echo and a pinger, and needs
        nothing but the virtual network: set a latency or a loss on the link and read the round trip in the inspector. A preset
        marked <em>full build</em> uses a node kind the loaded module does not carry yet; the badge disappears by itself once
        it does.
      </p>

      <div class="frosted-card rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-white/10 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-xs">
          <thead class="bg-white/5 text-left">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-gray-300">Control</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-gray-300">Effect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {controls.map((c) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-white/5">
                <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-krab-300 whitespace-nowrap align-top">{c.control}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-2 text-gray-300">{c.effect}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Saving, sharing, hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The scenario autosaves to this browser; the <strong class="text-white">Saved</strong> list reopens it with its durable
        state, and the <strong class="text-white">Storage</strong> panel shows what is kept per node and forgets it on request.
        <strong class="text-white">Export</strong> writes the scenario as a JSON file, <strong class="text-white">Copy link</strong>
        puts the whole document into the URL. Neither carries node state: a shared link starts the cluster fresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white">Invite a tab</strong> in the Session panel makes a link that another tab or another machine
        opens to join. That page shows an answer code; paste it back into the host and the two are connected without any server.
        The host then assigns nodes to peers from the inspector; frames between tabs travel over the WebRTC data channel, and each
        tab persists the state of the nodes it hosts.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Real brokers</h2>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-white">Krabka broker (real)</strong> in the palette adds the real
        <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">krabka-broker</code>, compiled for
        <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">wasm32-wasip1</code>, as a node of the lab. It runs in a Web Worker on a browser
        WASI runtime: its listeners, its dials to other nodes and its clock are the lab's, so links, faults, pause and speed
        apply to it like to any node, and its disk is a volume in this browser's IndexedDB. The runtime needs a
        cross-origin isolated page, so the first time a scenario with one runs here the page reloads once, and reopens the
        scenario. A real broker stays in the tab that runs it: its process cannot move to another tab of a session. Until the
        broker build is published on this site, the node says so and refuses connections.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">What is real</h2>
      <p>
        The simulation is <em>sans-IO</em>: the transport and the clock are the page's, everything above them is the code that
        runs in the broker and the clients, compiled unchanged for <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="frosted-card rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-white/10 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-xs">
          <thead class="bg-white/5 text-left">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-gray-300">Piece</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-gray-300">Crate</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-gray-300">Used for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {real.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-white/5">
                <td class="px-4 py-2 text-gray-300 align-top">{r.piece}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-krab-300 align-top whitespace-nowrap">{r.crate}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-2 text-gray-300 align-top">{r.use}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        What is simulated: the network (per-link latency, loss and cuts under the seed), disks (IndexedDB stands in for the log
        directory), and time. The lab makes no performance claims; the numbers on screen are counts and simulated milliseconds.
      </p>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose pt-2">
        <a href={getPath('/docs/playground')} class="p-4 rounded-xl frosted-card border border-white/10 hover:border-krab-500/50 transition-colors block">
          <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Consensus Playground →</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1">The KRaft quorum on its own, one message at a time.</p>
        </a>
        <a href={getPath('/verification')} class="p-4 rounded-xl frosted-card border border-white/10 hover:border-krab-500/50 transition-colors block">
          <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Correctness & Verification →</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1">How the broker you are simulating is proved and model-checked.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<script src={getPath('/playground/lab/app.js')} type="module" is:inline></script>
